  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nThe more impactful weather is expected on Sunday as the remnants \nof Rachel move through. According to the latest official forecast \ntrack from the National Hurricane Center, Rachel is expected to \naccelerate north-northeastward along the vicinity of the Lower \nColorado River Valley. An area of heavy rainfall is likely to \nfollow along and just to the west of where Rachel tracks. Even \nthough the official NHC forecast has the system tracking along the\nvicinity of the Lower Colorado River Valley suggesting that the \nheavy rainfall may fall across portions of southeast CA, there is \na lot of spread amongst the guidance in the track as some \nsolutions have Rachel tracking near the southern CA coast whereas \nother solutions have the system tracking as far east as southwest \nAZ. Therefore, the rainfall forecast is still highly volatile and \ncould still significantly shift depending on the eventual track. \nThe greater impact with Rachel will be the potential for strong  \nwinds as a belt of strong 850 mb winds in excess of 40-50 kts is \nlikely to set up along the eastern quadrant of Rachel with much \nof the guidance showing gusts exceeding 40-50 mph across much of \nsouthwest into south-central AZ, especially early Sunday morning \nthrough Sunday afternoon. Wind gusts this high could certainly \nresult in areas of blowing dust across the dust prone areas. \nGiven the strong wind potential, a wind advisory has been hoisted \nfrom eastern Riverside and Imperial Counties through all of \nsouthwest and south-central AZ. In addition to the wind threat, \nthere will also be the potential along the eastern quadrant for \nmultiple rounds of widely scattered showers and storms to \nmaterialize. There is a potential for some of these storms to \nreach severe levels given that strong vertical shear in excess of \n50 kts will be present, however, the severe potential will be \ndependent on how much instability will be present. \n\nEven though the remnants of Rachel will be well north of the area by \nSunday evening, unsettled conditions will continue right through \nearly next week as the main west coast trough moves through the \nregion bringing additional chances for showers and isolated \nthunderstorms, with the highest chances across the foothills and \nhigher terrain areas to the north and east of Phoenix from upslope \nflow. Thereafter, drier and quieter weather is expected by the \nmiddle of the week as the main trough axis lifts to the northeast. \n\nUnder the presence of the troughing pattern, well-below normal \ntemperatures are expected during the first half of next week with\nlower deserts highs going from the low to mid 80s on Sunday to \nmostly 70s Tuesday and Wednesday. Early morning lows will also \ntake a tumble with the coolest morning expected on Wednesday as \ntemperatures are expected to bottom out in the 50s across much of \nthe region with even some 40s across the higher terrain areas.",
